# Hand-written ambient declarations — the **/*.d.ts rule above targets build output.
#
# Every hand-written .d.ts needs a line here or git silently swallows it, and the
# failure is invisible locally: the file still exists on disk, so typecheck stays
# green in the worktree that created it and only goes red once that worktree is
# gone. tests/global.d.ts was lost exactly that way under TRA-1187, leaving main
# failing typecheck:tests with 34 errors against a gate that had never run on a
# tree git could actually produce. src/global.d.ts was deleted by that same work.
!tests/global.d.ts

# Git worktrees — Claude Code's native worktree tool writes here; this is the
# canonical location across trakrf repos. Narrow ignore: the rest of .claude/
# stays tracked so shared agent config is versioned.
.claude/worktrees/

# Superpowers design specs + implementation plans: generated per-session as
# working artifacts, never tracked. Both paths: the skills default to
# docs/superpowers/, and repos whose docs/ is published content use the root.
superpowers/
docs/superpowers/
.superpowers/

# Working notes — investigation records, scratch analysis, session notes. Same
# rationale as docs/superpowers/ above: they earn their keep while a ticket is
# being built and reviewed, then go stale fast (PIDs, bench state, host facts)
# while still reading as authoritative because they are checked in. Durable
# conclusions belong in CLAUDE.md, an ADR or a code comment; the narrative and
# evidence belong on the ticket.
docs/notes/

# Packed npm tarballs — build output, regenerated by `pnpm pack`
*.tgz
